WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.463
The authorizing official doesn't
read the evidence directly.

2
00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:07.933
They read a package that summarizes
it: the security and privacy plans,

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:12.249
the assessment reports, the plan of
action, and an executive summary.

4
00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:17.069
NIST says the package may be generated
from a tool, and FedRAMP's 2026

5
00:00:17.069 --> 00:00:21.975
rules now expect machine-readable
artifacts, at least OSCAL and JSON.

6
00:00:21.975 --> 00:00:26.703
In OSCAL the pieces link together: the
assessment plan points to the security plan,

7
00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:29.067
the results point to the assessment plan,

8
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:31.987
and the plan of action hangs
from the security plan.

9
00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:34.526
Every failing item gets a disposition.

10
00:00:34.526 --> 00:00:38.275
Real drift is an open item
with an owner and a deadline.

11
00:00:38.275 --> 00:00:43.450
An accepted exception carries its expiry,
and when that lapses, it's open again.

12
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.275
Nothing is always satisfied.

13
00:00:45.275 --> 00:00:47.871
And an empty plan of action
on a Moderate system

14
00:00:47.871 --> 00:00:50.800
should make you ask what the
assessment didn't look at.

15
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.660
Next, keeping it current.
